# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Um curso gratuito da IBM sobre informação e computação quântica"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Convex combinations of density matrices"
msgstr "Combinações convexas de matrizes de densidade"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"
msgstr "{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Probabilistic selections of density matrices {#probabilistic-selections-of-"
"density-matrices}"
msgstr ""
"Seleções probabilísticas de matrizes de densidade {#probabilistic-"
"selections-of-density-matrices}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A key aspect of density matrices is that *probabilistic selections* of "
"quantum states are represented by *convex combinations* of their associated "
"density matrices.\n"
msgstr ""
"Um aspecto fundamental das matrizes de densidade é que *seleções "
"probabilísticas* de estados quânticos são representadas por *combinações "
"convexas* das matrizes de densidade associadas a esses estados.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, if we have two density matrices, $\\rho$ and $\\sigma,$ "
"representing quantum states of a system $\\mathsf{X},$ and we prepare the "
"system in the state $\\rho$ with probability $p$ and $\\sigma$ with "
"probability $1 - p,$ then the resulting quantum state is represented by the "
"density matrix\n"
msgstr ""
"Por exemplo, se temos duas matrizes de densidade, $\\rho$ e $\\sigma,$ "
"representando estados quânticos de um sistema $\\mathsf{X},$ e preparamos o "
"sistema no estado $\\rho$ com probabilidade $p$ e no estado $\\sigma$ com "
"probabilidade $1 - p,$ então o estado quântico resultante é representado "
"pela matriz de densidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"p \\rho + (1 - p) \\sigma.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"p \\rho + (1 - p) \\sigma.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"More generally, if we have $m$ quantum states represented by density "
"matrices $\\rho_0,\\ldots,\\rho_{m-1},$ and a system is prepared in the "
"state $\\rho_k$ with probability $p_k$ for some probability vector "
"$(p_0,\\ldots,p_{m-1}),$ the resulting state is represented by the density "
"matrix\n"
msgstr ""
"De forma mais geral, se temos $m$ estados quânticos representados por "
"matrizes de densidade $\\rho_0,\\ldots,\\rho_{m-1},$ e um sistema é "
"preparado no estado $\\rho_k$ com probabilidade $p_k$ para algum vetor de "
"probabilidade $(p_0,\\ldots,p_{m-1}),$ o estado resultante é representado "
"pela matriz de densidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\rho_k.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\rho_k.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is a *convex combination* of the density matrices "
"$\\rho_0,\\ldots,\\rho_{m-1}.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Isso é uma *combinação convexa* das matrizes de densidade "
"$\\rho_0,\\ldots,\\rho_{m-1}.$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It follows that if we have $m$ quantum state vectors "
"$\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{m-1}\\rangle,$ and we prepare a "
"system in the state $\\vert\\psi_k\\rangle$ with probability $p_k$ for each "
"$k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\},$ the state we obtain is represented by the "
"density matrix\n"
msgstr ""
"Segue-se que se temos $m$ vetores de estado quântico "
"$\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{m-1}\\rangle,$ e preparamos um "
"sistema no estado $\\vert\\psi_k\\rangle$ com probabilidade $p_k$ para cada "
"$k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\},$ o estado obtido é representado pela matriz de "
"densidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\vert\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle\\psi_k\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\vert\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle\\psi_k\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, if a qubit is prepared in the state $\\vert 0\\rangle$ with "
"probability $1/2$ and in the state $\\vert + \\rangle$ with probability "
"$1/2,$ the density matrix representation of the state we obtain is given "
"by\n"
msgstr ""
"Por exemplo, se um qubit é preparado no estado $\\vert 0\\rangle$ com "
"probabilidade $1/2$ e no estado $\\vert + \\rangle$ com probabilidade $1/2,$"
" a representação em matriz de densidade do estado obtido é dada por\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the simplified formulation of quantum information, averaging quantum state vectors like this doesn't work.\n"
"For instance, the vector\n"
msgstr ""
"Na formulação simplificada da informação quântica, fazer a média de vetores de estado quântico dessa forma não funciona.\n"
"Por exemplo, o vetor\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert + \\rangle\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\[1mm] 0\\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\[2mm]\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert + \\rangle\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\[1mm] 0\\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\\\[2mm]\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\\\[2mm]\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"is not a valid quantum state vector because its Euclidean norm is not equal to $1.$\n"
"A more extreme example that shows that this doesn't work for quantum state vectors is that we fix any quantum state vector $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ that we wish, and then we take our state to be $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ with probability $1/2$ and $-\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ with probability $1/2.$\n"
"These states differ by a global phase, so they're actually the same state — but averaging gives us the zero vector, which is not a valid quantum state vector.\n"
msgstr ""
"não é um vetor de estado quântico válido porque sua norma euclidiana não é igual a $1.$\n"
"Um exemplo mais extremo que mostra que isso não funciona para vetores de estado quântico: fixamos qualquer vetor de estado quântico $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ que queiramos, e então tomamos nosso estado como sendo $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ com probabilidade $1/2$ e $-\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ com probabilidade $1/2.$\n"
"Esses estados diferem por uma fase global, então são na verdade o mesmo estado — mas fazer a média nos dá o vetor zero, que não é um vetor de estado quântico válido.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The completely mixed state {#the-completely-mixed-state}"
msgstr "O estado completamente misto {#the-completely-mixed-state}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Suppose we set the state of a qubit to be $\\vert 0\\rangle$ or $\\vert 1\\rangle$ randomly, each with probability $1/2.$\n"
"The density matrix representing the resulting state is as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Suponha que definimos o estado de um qubit como $\\vert 0\\rangle$ ou $\\vert 1\\rangle$ aleatoriamente, cada um com probabilidade $1/2.$\n"
"A matriz de densidade que representa o estado resultante é a seguinte.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & 1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(In this equation the symbol $\\mathbb{I}$ denotes the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix.)\n"
"This is a special state known as the *completely mixed state*.\n"
"It represents complete uncertainty about the state of a qubit, similar to a uniform random bit in the probabilistic setting.\n"
msgstr ""
"(Nessa equação, o símbolo $\\mathbb{I}$ denota a matriz identidade $2\\times 2$.)\n"
"Esse é um estado especial conhecido como o *estado completamente misto*.\n"
"Ele representa incerteza total sobre o estado de um qubit, semelhante a um bit aleatório uniforme no contexto probabilístico.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now suppose that we change the procedure: in place of the states $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle$ we'll use the states $\\vert + \\rangle$ and $\\vert - \\rangle.$\n"
"We can compute the density matrix that describes the resulting state in a similar way.\n"
msgstr ""
"Agora suponha que mudamos o procedimento: em vez dos estados $\\vert 0\\rangle$ e $\\vert 1\\rangle$, usaremos os estados $\\vert + \\rangle$ e $\\vert - \\rangle.$\n"
"Podemos calcular a matriz de densidade que descreve o estado resultante de forma similar.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle +\\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert -\\rangle\\langle -\\vert\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"=\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[2mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[2mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbb{I}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It's the same density matrix as before, even though we changed the states.\n"
"In fact, we would again obtain the same result — the completely mixed state — by substituting *any* two orthogonal qubit state vectors for $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
msgstr ""
"É a mesma matriz de densidade de antes, mesmo tendo mudado os estados.\n"
"Na verdade, obteríamos o mesmo resultado — o estado completamente misto — substituindo *quaisquer* dois vetores de estado de qubit ortogonais por $\\vert 0\\rangle$ e $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is a feature, not a bug!\n"
"We do in fact obtain exactly the same state either way.\n"
"That is, there's no way to distinguish the two procedures by measuring the qubit they produce, even in a statistical sense.\n"
"Our two different procedures are simply different ways to prepare this state.\n"
msgstr ""
"Isso é uma característica, não um bug!\n"
"De fato obtemos exatamente o mesmo estado de qualquer forma.\n"
"Ou seja, não há como distinguir os dois procedimentos medindo o qubit que eles produzem, nem mesmo de forma estatística.\n"
"Nossos dois procedimentos distintos são simplesmente formas diferentes de preparar esse estado.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can verify that this makes sense by thinking about what we could hope to learn given a random selection of a state from one of the two possible state sets $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$ and $\\{\\vert +\\rangle,\\vert -\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"To keep things simple, let's suppose that we perform a unitary operation $U$ on our qubit and then measure in the standard basis.\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos verificar que isso faz sentido pensando no que poderíamos esperar aprender dado uma seleção aleatória de um estado de um dos dois possíveis conjuntos de estados $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}$ e $\\{\\vert +\\rangle,\\vert -\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"Para simplificar, vamos supor que realizamos uma operação unitária $U$ em nosso qubit e depois medimos na base padrão.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the first scenario, the state of the qubit is chosen uniformly from the set $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"If the state is $\\vert 0\\rangle,$ we obtain the outcomes $0$ and $1$ with probabilities\n"
msgstr ""
"No primeiro cenário, o estado do qubit é escolhido uniformemente do conjunto $\\{\\vert 0\\rangle,\\vert 1\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"Se o estado é $\\vert 0\\rangle,$ obtemos os resultados $0$ e $1$ com probabilidades\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\quad\\text{e}\\quad\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"respectively.\n"
"If the state is $\\vert 1\\rangle,$ we obtain the outcomes $0$ and $1$ with probabilities\n"
msgstr ""
"respectivamente.\n"
"Se o estado é $\\vert 1\\rangle,$ obtemos os resultados $0$ e $1$ com probabilidades\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\quad\\text{e}\\quad\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because the two possibilities each happen with probability $1/2,$ we obtain "
"the outcome $0$ with probability\n"
msgstr ""
"Como as duas possibilidades ocorrem cada uma com probabilidade $1/2,$ "
"obtemos o resultado $0$ com probabilidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "and the outcome $1$ with probability\n"
msgstr "e o resultado $1$ com probabilidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both of these expressions are equal to $1/2.$\n"
"One way to argue this is to use a fact from linear algebra that can be seen as a generalization of the Pythagorean theorem.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ambas as expressões são iguais a $1/2.$\n"
"Uma forma de argumentar isso é usar um resultado de álgebra linear que pode ser visto como uma generalização do teorema de Pitágoras.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Theorem\">\n"
"Suppose $\\{\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_n\\rangle\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of a (real or complex) vector space $\\mathcal{V}.$ For every vector $\\vert \\phi\\rangle \\in \\mathcal{V}$ we have\n"
"$\\vert \\langle \\psi_1\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\vert^2 + \\cdots + \\vert \\langle \\psi_n \\vert \\phi \\rangle\\vert^2 = \\| \\vert\\phi\\rangle \\|^2.$\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Teorema\">\n"
"Suponha que $\\{\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_n\\rangle\\}$ é uma base ortonormal de um espaço vetorial (real ou complexo) $\\mathcal{V}.$ Para todo vetor $\\vert \\phi\\rangle \\in \\mathcal{V}$ temos\n"
"$\\vert \\langle \\psi_1\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\vert^2 + \\cdots + \\vert \\langle \\psi_n \\vert \\phi \\rangle\\vert^2 = \\| \\vert\\phi\\rangle \\|^2.$\n"
"</Figure>\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can apply this theorem to determine the probabilities as follows.\n"
"The probability to get $0$ is\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos aplicar esse teorema para determinar as probabilidades da seguinte forma.\n"
"A probabilidade de obter $0$ é\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr) \\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr) \\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "and the probability to get $1$ is\n"
msgstr "e a probabilidade de obter $1$ é\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr) \\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 0 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr) \\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\Bigl(\n"
"\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2.\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because $U$ is unitary, we know that $U^{\\dagger}$ is unitary as well, implying that both $U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle$ and $U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle$ are unit vectors.\n"
"Both probabilities are therefore equal to $1/2.$\n"
"This means that no matter how we choose $U,$ we're just going to get a uniform random bit from the measurement.\n"
msgstr ""
"Como $U$ é unitário, sabemos que $U^{\\dagger}$ também é unitário, o que implica que tanto $U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle$ quanto $U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle$ são vetores unitários.\n"
"Ambas as probabilidades são portanto iguais a $1/2.$\n"
"Isso significa que independentemente de como escolhemos $U,$ vamos simplesmente obter um bit aleatório uniforme da medição.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can perform a similar verification for any other pair of orthonormal states in place of $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"For example, because $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$ is an orthonormal basis, the probability to obtain the measurement outcome $0$ in the second procedure is\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos realizar uma verificação similar para qualquer outro par de estados ortonormais no lugar de $\\vert 0\\rangle$ e $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"Por exemplo, como $\\{\\vert + \\rangle, \\vert - \\rangle\\}$ é uma base ortonormal, a probabilidade de obter o resultado de medição $0$ no segundo procedimento é\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert + \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert - \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert + \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 0 \\vert U \\vert - \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 0 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert + \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert - \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert + \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"+ \\frac{1}{2}\\vert \\langle 1 \\vert U \\vert - \\rangle \\vert^2\n"
"= \\frac{1}{2} \\bigl\\| U^{\\dagger} \\vert 1 \\rangle \\bigr\\|^2 = \\frac{1}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In particular, we obtain exactly the same output statistics as we did for "
"the states $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Em particular, obtemos exatamente as mesmas estatísticas de saída que "
"obtivemos para os estados $\\vert 0\\rangle$ e $\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Probabilistic states {#probabilistic-states}"
msgstr "Estados probabilísticos {#probabilistic-states}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Classical states can be represented by density matrices.\n"
"In particular, for each classical state $a$ of a system $\\mathsf{X},$ the density matrix\n"
msgstr ""
"Estados clássicos podem ser representados por matrizes de densidade.\n"
"Em particular, para cada estado clássico $a$ de um sistema $\\mathsf{X},$ a matriz de densidade\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\vert a\\rangle \\langle a \\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\vert a\\rangle \\langle a \\vert\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"represents $\\mathsf{X}$ being definitively in the classical state $a.$\n"
"For qubits we have\n"
msgstr ""
"representa $\\mathsf{X}$ estando definitivamente no estado clássico $a.$\n"
"Para qubits temos\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert 0\\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\vert 1\\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert 0\\rangle \\langle 0 \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\quad\\text{e}\\quad\n"
"\\vert 1\\rangle \\langle 1 \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"and in general we have a single $1$ on the diagonal in the position "
"corresponding to the classical state we have in mind, with all other entries"
" zero.\n"
msgstr ""
"e em geral temos um único $1$ na diagonal na posição correspondente ao "
"estado clássico que temos em mente, com todas as demais entradas iguais a "
"zero.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can then take convex combinations of these density matrices to represent probabilistic states.\n"
"Supposing for simplicity that our classical state set is $\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\},$ if  $\\mathsf{X}$ is in the state $a$ with probability $p_a$ for each $a\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\},$ then the density matrix we obtain is\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos então tomar combinações convexas dessas matrizes de densidade para representar estados probabilísticos.\n"
"Supondo por simplicidade que nosso conjunto de estados clássicos é $\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\},$ se $\\mathsf{X}$ está no estado $a$ com probabilidade $p_a$ para cada $a\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n-1\\},$ então a matriz de densidade que obtemos é\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{a = 0}^{n-1} p_a \\vert a\\rangle \\langle a \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"p_0 & 0 & \\cdots & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & p_1 & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\\n"
"\\vdots & \\ddots & \\ddots & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & \\cdots & 0 & p_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{a = 0}^{n-1} p_a \\vert a\\rangle \\langle a \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"p_0 & 0 & \\cdots & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & p_1 & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\\n"
"\\vdots & \\ddots & \\ddots & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & \\cdots & 0 & p_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Going in the other direction, any diagonal density matrix can naturally be "
"identified with the probabilistic state we obtain by simply reading the "
"probability vector off from the diagonal.\n"
msgstr ""
"No sentido inverso, qualquer matriz de densidade diagonal pode ser "
"naturalmente identificada com o estado probabilístico que obtemos "
"simplesmente lendo o vetor de probabilidades a partir da diagonal.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be clear, when a density matrix is diagonal, it's not necessarily the "
"case that we're talking about a classical system, or that the system must "
"have been prepared through the random selection of a classical state, but "
"rather that the state *could* have been obtained through the random "
"selection of a classical state.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para ficar claro, quando uma matriz de densidade é diagonal, não é "
"necessariamente o caso de que estamos falando de um sistema clássico, ou que"
" o sistema deva ter sido preparado por meio da seleção aleatória de um "
"estado clássico, mas sim que o estado *poderia* ter sido obtido por meio da "
"seleção aleatória de um estado clássico.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The fact that probabilistic states are represented by diagonal density matrices is consistent with the intuition suggested at the start of the lesson that off-diagonal entries describe the degree to which the two classical states corresponding to the row and column of that entry are in quantum superposition.\n"
"Here, all of the off-diagonal entries are zero, so we just have classical randomness and nothing is in quantum superposition.\n"
msgstr ""
"O fato de que estados probabilísticos são representados por matrizes de densidade diagonais é consistente com a intuição sugerida no início da lição de que as entradas fora da diagonal descrevem o grau em que os dois estados clássicos correspondentes à linha e à coluna daquela entrada estão em superposição quântica.\n"
"Aqui, todas as entradas fora da diagonal são zero, portanto temos apenas aleatoriedade clássica e nada está em superposição quântica.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Density matrices and the spectral theorem {#density-matrices-and-the-"
"spectral-theorem}"
msgstr ""
"Matrizes de densidade e o teorema espectral {#density-matrices-and-the-"
"spectral-theorem}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We've seen that if we take a convex combination of pure states,\n"
msgstr "Vimos que se tomarmos uma combinação convexa de estados puros,\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle \\langle \\psi_k \\vert,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\sum_{k = 0}^{m-1} p_k \\vert \\psi_k\\rangle \\langle \\psi_k \\vert,\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"we obtain a density matrix.\n"
"Every density matrix $\\rho,$ in fact, can be expressed as a convex combination of pure states like this.\n"
"That is, there will always exist a collection of unit vectors $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{m-1}\\rangle\\}$ and a probability vector $(p_0,\\ldots,p_{m-1})$ for which the equation above is true.\n"
msgstr ""
"obtemos uma matriz de densidade.\n"
"Toda matriz de densidade $\\rho,$ na verdade, pode ser expressa como uma combinação convexa de estados puros dessa forma.\n"
"Ou seja, sempre existirá uma coleção de vetores unitários $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{m-1}\\rangle\\}$ e um vetor de probabilidade $(p_0,\\ldots,p_{m-1})$ para os quais a equação acima é verdadeira.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can, moreover, always choose the number $m$ so that it agrees with the number of classical states of the system being considered, and we can select the quantum state vectors to be orthogonal.\n"
"The spectral theorem, which we encountered in the \"Foundations of quantum algorithms\" course, allows us to conclude this.\n"
"Here's a restatement of the spectral theorem for convenience.\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos, além disso, sempre escolher o número $m$ de modo que ele coincida com o número de estados clássicos do sistema em questão, e podemos selecionar os vetores de estado quântico de forma que sejam ortogonais.\n"
"O teorema espectral, que encontramos no curso \"Fundamentos de algoritmos quânticos\", nos permite concluir isso.\n"
"Aqui está uma reapresentação do teorema espectral para referência.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Theorem\">\n"
"Spectral theorem: Let $M$ be a *normal* $n\\times n$ complex matrix.\n"
"There exists an orthonormal basis of $n$ dimensional complex vectors $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{n-1}\\rangle \\}$ along with complex numbers $\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1}$ such that\n"
"$$\n"
"M = \\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Teorema\">\n"
"Teorema espectral: Seja $M$ uma matriz complexa *normal* de ordem $n\\times n$.\n"
"Existe uma base ortonormal de vetores complexos $n$-dimensionais $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{n-1}\\rangle \\}$ juntamente com números complexos $\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1}$ tais que\n"
"$$\n"
"M = \\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
"</Figure>\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(Recall that a matrix $M$ is *normal* if it satisfies $M^{\\dagger} M = M "
"M^{\\dagger}.$ In words, normal matrices are matrices that commute with "
"their own conjugate transpose.)\n"
msgstr ""
"(Lembre que uma matriz $M$ é *normal* se satisfaz $M^{\\dagger} M = M "
"M^{\\dagger}.$ Em palavras, matrizes normais são matrizes que comutam com "
"sua própria transposta conjugada.)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can apply the spectral theorem to any given density matrix $\\rho$ because density matrices are always Hermitian and therefore normal.\n"
"This allows us to write\n"
msgstr ""
"Podemos aplicar o teorema espectral a qualquer matriz de densidade $\\rho$ dada, pois matrizes de densidade são sempre Hermitianas e portanto normais.\n"
"Isso nos permite escrever\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for some orthonormal basis $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"It remains to verify that $(\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1})$ is a probability vector, which we can then rename to $(p_0,\\ldots,p_{n-1})$ if we wish.\n"
msgstr ""
"para alguma base ortonormal $\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert\\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\}.$\n"
"Resta verificar que $(\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1})$ é um vetor de probabilidade, que podemos então renomear como $(p_0,\\ldots,p_{n-1})$ se quisermos.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The numbers $\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1}$ are the eigenvalues of $\\rho,$ and because $\\rho$ is positive semidefinite, these numbers must therefore be nonnegative real numbers.\n"
"We can conclude that $\\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} = 1$ from the fact that $\\rho$ has trace equal to $1.$\n"
"Going through the details will give us an opportunity to point out the following important and very useful property of the trace.\n"
msgstr ""
"Os números $\\lambda_0,\\ldots,\\lambda_{n-1}$ são os autovalores de $\\rho,$ e como $\\rho$ é semidefinida positiva, esses números devem portanto ser números reais não negativos.\n"
"Podemos concluir que $\\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} = 1$ a partir do fato de que $\\rho$ tem traço igual a $1.$\n"
"Percorrer os detalhes nos dará a oportunidade de destacar a seguinte propriedade importante e muito útil do traço.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Figure title=\"Theorem\">\n"
"Cyclic property of the trace: For any two matrices $A$ and $B$ that give us a square matrix $AB$ by multiplying, the equality $\\operatorname{Tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{Tr}(BA)$ is true.\n"
"</Figure>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Figure title=\"Teorema\">\n"
"Propriedade cíclica do traço: Para quaisquer duas matrizes $A$ e $B$ que nos dão uma matriz quadrada $AB$ ao multiplicar, a igualdade $\\operatorname{Tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{Tr}(BA)$ é verdadeira.\n"
"</Figure>\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that this theorem works even if $A$ and $B$ are not themselves square matrices.\n"
"That is, we may have that $A$ is $n\\times m$ and $B$ is $m\\times n,$ for some choice of positive integers $n$ and $m,$ so that $AB$ is an $n\\times n$ square matrix and $BA$ is $m\\times m.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Note que esse teorema funciona mesmo quando $A$ e $B$ não são individualmente matrizes quadradas.\n"
"Ou seja, podemos ter $A$ de ordem $n\\times m$ e $B$ de ordem $m\\times n,$ para alguma escolha de inteiros positivos $n$ e $m,$ de modo que $AB$ é uma matriz quadrada $n\\times n$ e $BA$ é uma matriz $m\\times m.$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In particular, if we let $A$ be a column vector $\\vert\\phi\\rangle$ and "
"let $B$ be the row vector $\\langle \\phi\\vert,$ then we see that\n"
msgstr ""
"Em particular, se deixarmos $A$ ser um vetor coluna $\\vert\\phi\\rangle$ e "
"$B$ ser o vetor linha $\\langle \\phi\\vert,$ então vemos que\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\langle\\phi\\vert\\bigr)\n"
"= \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\bigr) = \\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\langle\\phi\\vert\\bigr)\n"
"= \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle\\bigr) = \\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second equality follows from the fact that $\\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle$ is a scalar, which we can also think of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix whose trace is its single entry.\n"
"Using this fact, we can conclude that $\\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} = 1$ by the linearity of the trace function.\n"
msgstr ""
"A segunda igualdade segue do fato de que $\\langle\\phi\\vert\\phi\\rangle$ é um escalar, que podemos também pensar como uma matriz $1\\times 1$ cujo traço é sua única entrada.\n"
"Usando esse fato, podemos concluir que $\\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} = 1$ pela linearidade da função traço.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"1 = \\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho) =\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\\bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"= \\lambda_0 \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert\\bigr) + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\\bigr)\n"
"= \\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{gathered}\n"
"1 = \\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho) =\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\lambda_0 \\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\\bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n"
"= \\lambda_0 \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert \\psi_0\\rangle\\langle \\psi_0\\vert\\bigr) + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1} \\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(\\vert \\psi_{n-1}\\rangle\\langle \\psi_{n-1}\\vert\\bigr)\n"
"= \\lambda_0 + \\cdots + \\lambda_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{gathered}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Alternatively, we can reach the same conclusion by using the fact that the "
"trace of a square matrix (even one that isn't normal) is equal to the sum of"
" its eigenvalues.\n"
msgstr ""
"Alternativamente, podemos chegar à mesma conclusão usando o fato de que o "
"traço de uma matriz quadrada (mesmo uma que não seja normal) é igual à soma "
"de seus autovalores.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We have therefore concluded that any given density matrix $\\rho$ can be expressed as a convex combination of pure states.\n"
"We also see that we can, moreover, take the pure states to be *orthogonal*.\n"
"This means, in particular, that we never need the number $n$ to be larger than the size of the classical state set of $\\mathsf{X}.$\n"
msgstr ""
"Concluímos, portanto, que qualquer matriz de densidade $\\rho$ dada pode ser expressa como uma combinação convexa de estados puros.\n"
"Vemos também que podemos, além disso, tomar os estados puros como sendo *ortogonais*.\n"
"Isso significa, em particular, que nunca precisamos que o número $n$ seja maior que o tamanho do conjunto de estados clássicos de $\\mathsf{X}.$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In general, it must be understood that there will be different ways to write a density matrix as a convex combination of pure states, not just the ways that the spectral theorem provides.\n"
"A previous example illustrates this.\n"
msgstr ""
"Em geral, deve-se entender que haverá diferentes formas de escrever uma matriz de densidade como uma combinação convexa de estados puros, não apenas as formas que o teorema espectral fornece.\n"
"Um exemplo anterior ilustra isso.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0 \\vert + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert +\\rangle\\langle + \\vert\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is not a spectral decomposition of this matrix because $\\vert 0\\rangle$ and $\\vert + \\rangle$ are not orthogonal.\n"
"Here's a spectral decomposition:\n"
msgstr ""
"Esta não é uma decomposição espectral dessa matriz porque $\\vert 0\\rangle$ e $\\vert + \\rangle$ não são ortogonais.\n"
"Aqui está uma decomposição espectral:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\cos^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{\\pi/8}\\vert +\n"
"\\sin^2(\\pi/8) \\vert \\psi_{5\\pi/8} \\rangle \\langle \\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\vert,\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\vert \\psi_{\\theta} \\rangle = \\cos(\\theta)\\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\theta)\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"The eigenvalues are numbers that will likely look familiar:\n"
msgstr ""
"onde $\\vert \\psi_{\\theta} \\rangle = \\cos(\\theta)\\vert 0\\rangle + \\sin(\\theta)\\vert 1\\rangle.$\n"
"Os autovalores são números que provavelmente parecerão familiares:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\cos^2(\\pi/8) = \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\approx 0.85 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\sin^2(\\pi/8) = \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\approx 0.15.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\cos^2(\\pi/8) = \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\approx 0.85 \\quad\\text{e}\\quad \\sin^2(\\pi/8) = \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\approx 0.15.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The eigenvectors can be written explicitly like this.\n"
msgstr "Os autovetores podem ser escritos explicitamente assim.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle & =\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle \\\\[3mm]\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle & =\n"
"  -\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{\\pi/8}\\rangle & =\n"
"  \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle \\\\[3mm]\n"
"  \\vert\\psi_{5\\pi/8}\\rangle & =\n"
"  -\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 0\\rangle + \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As another, more general example, suppose $\\vert \\phi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert \\phi_{99} \\rangle$ are quantum state vectors representing states of a single qubit, chosen arbitrarily — so we're not assuming any particular relationships among these vectors.\n"
"We could then consider the state we obtain by choosing one of these $100$ states uniformly at random:\n"
msgstr ""
"Como outro exemplo mais geral, suponha que $\\vert \\phi_0\\rangle,\\ldots,\\vert \\phi_{99} \\rangle$ são vetores de estado quântico representando estados de um único qubit, escolhidos arbitrariamente — portanto não estamos assumindo nenhuma relação particular entre esses vetores.\n"
"Poderíamos então considerar o estado que obtemos escolhendo um desses $100$ estados uniformemente ao acaso:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\frac{1}{100} \\sum_{k = 0}^{99} \\vert \\phi_k\\rangle\\langle \\phi_k \\vert.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\frac{1}{100} \\sum_{k = 0}^{99} \\vert \\phi_k\\rangle\\langle \\phi_k \\vert.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because we're talking about a qubit, the density matrix $\\rho$ is $2\\times"
" 2,$ so by the spectral theorem we could alternatively write\n"
msgstr ""
"Como estamos falando de um qubit, a matriz de densidade $\\rho$ é $2\\times "
"2,$ então pelo teorema espectral poderíamos alternativamente escrever\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = p \\vert\\psi_0\\rangle\\langle\\psi_0\\vert + (1 - p) \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle\\langle\\psi_1\\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = p \\vert\\psi_0\\rangle\\langle\\psi_0\\vert + (1 - p) \\vert\\psi_1\\rangle\\langle\\psi_1\\vert\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for some real number $p\\in[0,1]$ and an orthonormal basis "
"$\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle\\}$ — but naturally the "
"existence of this expression doesn't prohibit us from writing $\\rho$ as an "
"average of 100 pure states if we choose to do that.\n"
msgstr ""
"para algum número real $p\\in[0,1]$ e uma base ortonormal "
"$\\{\\vert\\psi_0\\rangle,\\vert\\psi_1\\rangle\\}$ — mas naturalmente a "
"existência dessa expressão não nos proíbe de escrever $\\rho$ como uma média"
" de 100 estados puros caso optemos por fazer isso.\n"
